594 0 obj
<<
/Title (Copilot  Approach: Microsoft Copilot took a more concise and organized approach. It separated the discussion into arguments in favour of copyright protection, arguments against it, and a possible middle ground. This made the response easy to understand and follow.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 584 0 R
/Next 596 0 R
>>
endobj
595 0 obj
<<
/Title (Its reasoning is built around several major concepts, including human authorship, market flooding, training data, and the difference between effort and originality. One particularly interesting part is its discussion of the "sweat of the brow" argument. It distinguishes between spending time or money on generating content and actually contributing original expression.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 597 0 R
/Next 585 0 R
>>
endobj
596 0 obj
<<
/Title (Copilot also recognized the difference between completely autonomous AI creations and works where humans guide, edit, or modify the AI output. It considered both sides of the issue instead of presenting only one argument, which reflects the critical-thinking principle of considering different perspectives.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 594 0 R
/Next 598 0 R
>>
endobj
597 0 obj
<<
/Title (Gemini  Approach: Gemini uses a much more legal, economic, and philosophical approach. It frames copyright as a system intended to protect human creative labour and argues that extending protection to autonomous AI outputs could change the purpose of copyright itself.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 599 0 R
/Next 595 0 R
>>
endobj
598 0 obj
<<
/Title (Compared with ChatGPT and Gemini, however, Copilot did not explore each argument in as much depth. It identified the important points but generally provided less explanation about their wider consequences. Its main strength was therefore its clarity and organization rather than the depth of its analysis.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 596 0 R
/First 600 0 R
/Last 601 0 R
/Count -3
/Next 599 0 R
>>
endobj
